-\section{Limitations}
+\section{Discussion and Limitations}
 \label{sec:limitations}
 
-This paper mainly focuses on inference-time adapter scaling and does not retrain the base multi-view diffusion model or the adapter. Therefore, TCAS can alleviate texture degradation under high scales---which may manifest as flattening or artifact amplification depending on the adapter's residual properties---but it cannot fundamentally solve the limitations of the base model in invisible-region completion, complex material synthesis, and cross-view detail consistency.
-
-Second, Laplacian variance, RGB standard deviation, and gradient magnitude are proxy metrics for texture degradation. They are effective for detecting high-frequency loss and surface smoothing, but they cannot fully replace human perceptual evaluation. For objects with strong reflection, transparency, thin structures, or highly stylized textures, discrepancies may exist between these metrics and subjective quality.
-
-In addition, the current TCAS uses fixed stage boundaries and a fixed scale combination, C3 = (1.25, 2.50, 1.25). This setting is simple and stable, but it should be understood as a validated inference strategy for the studied geometry-adapter pipeline rather than a universally optimal schedule. As shown in Section~\ref{subsec:adapterdep}, the failure mode of high-scale intervention is adapter-dependent, and even the most sensitive denoising stage differs across adapters; yet C3's middle-stage concentration was the best-performing schedule among all tested variants in every regime examined, suggesting that its temporal structure is robust even when specific failure mechanisms vary. It has not yet been adapted to object complexity, material type, input reference image quality, or different sampler families, and the schedule has not yet been validated on a different geometry-conditioned multi-view diffusion backbone. Future work could study content-adaptive stage boundaries, SNR-aware scaling, and learned scale selection strategies.
-
-Finally, our controlled re-examination of the learned FAC extension indicates that unconstrained lightweight gating can flatten the temporal envelope that makes TCAS effective; learned modulation is therefore promising only under envelope-preserving constraints, which we identify as a concrete direction for future work, while TCAS remains the main training-free method.
-
+\paragraph{Strength and timing answer different questions}
+A low--high--low curve can appear effective when the fixed comparator is weak, yet that contrast changes both amplitude and placement. Fresh C makes this distinction concrete: stronger competitive controls change the practical recommendation, and the preserved equal-budget primary does not support the earlier middle-window advantage. Separate pairwise results favor Fixed Mean and Late-high over C3 on several endpoints, while Late-high trades lower LPIPS and higher PSNR against lower FG-SSIM than Fixed Mean. These observations change the tested recommendation, but do not show that every temporal schedule is ineffective or that Late-high is universally best.
+
+\paragraph{Matching scalar sums does not identify a residual-dose mechanism}
+The native shallow cap makes all three C3 requests identical after clipping in that group. A requested-profile comparison can therefore describe an intervention that was never applied as drawn. Reporting the implemented per-group profiles and sums removes that ambiguity. Matching those sums still leaves the adapter activation and hidden-state trajectory free to change. The matched-budget contrasts estimate the total effect of allocating the scalar control, including the resulting correction changes; they do not isolate timing at an equal realized residual dose. Raw correction norms also depend on tensor dimensions. The descriptive activation traces in Supplementary Section~S1 cannot validate a universal stage mechanism or assign a structural/texture function to each cap group.
+
+\paragraph{What baking adds to the interpretation}
+Generated-view metrics are informative: broad within-object rankings are positively associated with baked rankings. However, projection and texture construction add losses, and the minimum-score control can change between image and asset endpoints. The association weakens in GSO when restricted to closely competing scale/time controls. These are associations and changes of point-estimate winners, not statistically resolved rank reversals.
+
+In the compatible GeoTex pilot, Late-high reduces LPIPS by about 2\% relative to Fixed Mean but closes only 3.7\% of its gap to the matched GT bake. The latter is a reference for projection/resampling loss, not a theoretical ceiling; GT error is not subtracted from method errors. Reference-color and detail discrepancies remain visible, and the separate GSO protocol does not detect the same allocation benefit. Thus the asset evidence quantifies a local control effect alongside the much larger remaining appearance problem. Incoming-view disagreement is also a distinct diagnostic: a constant wrong color can have zero variance. Neither this diagnostic nor unlit LPIPS certifies seams, physical albedo or PBR/material fidelity.
+
+\paragraph{What can and cannot transfer}
+The native MV-Adapter50 and GSO100 primary intervals do not detect a middle-window advantage; they do not establish equivalence. The secondary GSO late-window PSNR effect is small and has no detected nonreference LPIPS counterpart. A reference-conditioned GeoTex checkpoint gives a different Late--Mean direction, and MVDiffusion tests only a partial correspondence operator. These observations limit a portable middle-stage rule. Because inputs, weights, operators and sampling/projection protocols change together, the studies cannot attribute their response differences to a backbone or domain alone. Equal numerical scale labels across them are not common physical doses.
+
+\paragraph{Consequences for evaluating a texturing control}
+A practical comparison should first retain the deployed input and camera contract and compare native/default and competitive fixed settings. To interpret placement, it should report the cap-adjusted profiles and match per-group applied sums, with residual traces used to describe rather than certify dose. Reference-color errors and full-object displays should accompany image metrics. Actual baking with shared meshes, unseen cameras and a matched reference bake can then assess whether the effect matters for the asset. Finally, fixed-seed differences should be examined at object level: positive cohort means can coexist with mixed directions across seeds. This is an evaluation sequence supported by the observed failure of stronger interpretations, not a newly validated selection algorithm. The evidence gives no general reason to replace a deployment's fixed setting with C3.
+
+\paragraph{Selection, statistics and replication limits}
+The bake cohorts favor compatible native-UV assets and GSO uses a source-ordered convenience sample. The two bake studies have different conditioning/projection protocols; the revised GSO calibration followed inspection of initial outcomes. Equal-budget analyses were specified on an already evaluated Fresh C cohort and remain retrospective; the dominance, seed and coverage addenda are descriptive. Separate multiplicity families and pointwise intervals are not one overall error guarantee. Views and seeds remain nested within object, and three fixed seeds do not estimate training-checkpoint variation.
+
+Fresh C uses controlled retraining and sampling rather than the authenticated historical main checkpoint or the complete default pipeline. The original $+0.96$ dB result is not reproduced, and the historical-request panel has an unknown substituted input, precluding a verified 276-object holdout interpretation. Original preference evidence concerns its own stimuli; the historical FAC manifest and weights are incomplete. These limits are retained in Supplementary Sections~S6--S8. They constrain historical replication and deployment claims while leaving the paired within-protocol comparisons interpretable.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 
-This paper presents a systematic experimental analysis of adapter scaling in multi-view diffusion texture generation. The experiments show that higher adapter scales continuously improve structural metrics such as FG-SSIM, but such improvement can mask texture quality degradation. In the 26-object texture audit, 92\% of objects show texture flattening and high-frequency detail loss under $s = 2.50$. This reveals an adapter-specific temporal conflict that is not captured by simply treating the scale as a generic guidance strength.
-
-To address this shape-texture trade-off, we propose Timestep-Conditioned Adapter Scaling (TCAS). The selected configuration, C3, applies strong adapter correction in the middle denoising stage and uses conservative scales in the early and late stages. The 24-object probe set is used only to select C3 from 17 scaling variants that include uniform, layer-wise, and temporal alternatives. With this schedule fixed, the 300-object evaluation pool further shows that C3 achieves FG-SSIM comparable to aggressive uniform scaling $s = 2.50$ while improving PSNR by 0.96~dB.
-
-Therefore, the core contribution of this paper is not to claim that the generated results are visually close to the ground truth in all cases, nor to present TCAS as a universal replacement for all guidance schedules. Instead, the contribution is to reveal and validate a hidden shape-texture trade-off caused by geometry-adapter residual injection, and to show that a simple non-monotonic inference schedule can mitigate this trade-off better than fixed high-scale control and other tested scheduling variants. This conclusion suggests that, in the geometry-controlled multi-view pipeline studied here---and plausibly in similar geometry-conditioned texture pipelines---adapter strength is better treated as a stage-dependent inference control variable than as a single fixed hyperparameter.
-
-As an additional check, a controlled re-examination of the learned-correction extension (FAC) under a strictly paired protocol showed that unconstrained lightweight gating does not preserve the schedule's shape; the analysis delimits the scenario in which learned modulation helps---only under envelope-preserving constraints---and the training-free TCAS remains the main and sole method.
+Geometry-control allocation in multi-view texturing must be interpreted at the implemented interface and the final asset endpoint. On the held-out GeoTex cohort, competitive baselines and cap-adjusted budgets change the interpretation of C3's gain over Fixed Low. The specified matched-budget primary does not support a middle-window advantage, while separate pairwise contrasts reveal endpoint-dependent placement effects. Native-UV baking measures a small Late-high benefit on ten compatible objects together with persistent appearance error; a separate GSO study does not detect this benefit. Native MV-Adapter comparisons also do not establish the proposed middle-window gain. The resulting knowledge is a bounded account of what these control comparisons identify and how their outcomes relate to textured meshes. It supports stronger comparison and asset-evaluation decisions, without a universal schedule, independently derived stage mechanism or faithful material reproduction.
 
 \section*{Data availability}
 
-All data included in this study are available upon request by contact with the corresponding author.
+The accompanying research package contains object-level metrics, frozen protocols and configuration records, analysis scripts, and the LaTeX source needed to rebuild the manuscript and Supplement. Recovered FAC source is supplied with a record of its entry points and validation scope; these component checks do not constitute a historical full-model replay. Statistical summaries can be recomputed without model weights, while fresh inference requires the recorded weights, runtime and licensed source assets. Raw generated-image archives, trained checkpoints and participant-level responses are not included. Source assets must be acquired under their recorded licenses. Additional analysis records may be requested from the corresponding author, subject to licensing, privacy and archive constraints.
 
